	INDICATOR 1 OF 6
Percentile of Publication Output
How much a researcher has published compared with their peers.
What it shows
	The percentile rank of a researcher’s publication output compared with their peers.
	Turns orange when
	Never. This indicator is always grey and is shown for context only.
	Source
	Publications assigned to researcher profiles by Dimensions, and Dimensions’ field of research classification. Includes preprints, books and book chapters.
	HOW IT WORKS
Peers are all researchers with the same main research field and the same first publication year. A researcher’s main research field is the most frequently detected level 2 ANZSRC field of research in their publications.
For example, “Top 2% compared to other researchers in the same main research field (‘Pure Mathematics’) and over a similar publishing period (since 2013)” means the researcher is in the top 2% of all pure mathematicians who started publishing in 2013. In other words, 98% of pure mathematicians who started in 2013 published less.
RESEARCHERS IN MORE THAN ONE FIELD
If a researcher publishes in philosophy and physics, but physics authors publish more papers, the percentile is shown among physicists.
	

An early career researcher normally publishes less than a senior academic, and in some fields, researchers publish less than in others. This is considered in the calculations. Percentiles are shown in a similar way for other indicators, to give a feel for how “unusual” a researcher’s number of publications is.
WHY IT MATTERS
A researcher with a high output percentile is extremely prolific compared with other researchers of the same “academic age” and field. This may be due to their productivity and skill. However, it could also be due to the use of paper mills or other unethical publication practices.
INDICATOR 2 OF 6
Flagged Publications
The number of a researcher’s publications associated with an atypical event, such as a retraction or an expression of concern.
What it shows
	The count and details of a researcher’s atypical publications.
	Turns orange when
	The researcher has at least one flagged publication.
	Sources
	See Section 3, Where the Data Comes From.
	WHEN A PUBLICATION HAS MORE THAN ONE FLAG
Many publications have flags from more than one source. For example, tortured phrases may be detected, then lead to an expression of concern, and later to a retraction reported to PubMed and Retraction Watch. In those cases the most serious flag with the most metadata is highlighted (here, the retraction reported to Retraction Watch). All other flags appear when you click the flag in the flagged publications table on the researcher profile page.
WHY IT MATTERS, AND WHAT TO KEEP IN MIND
Researchers with problematic behavior in the past may also use unethical means to increase their publication output in the future. However, care must be taken with retractions:
* Honest mistakes count in a researcher’s favor. Researchers may have made honest mistakes, may agree with the retraction, or may have reported a problem with their paper themselves. This should be counted as a positive.
* Not every retraction signals wrongdoing. Some retractions are due to certain publication procedures, or to third-party rights issues with an image.
* Many problems are never reported. The retraction process can be long and complicated, and many problematic papers have not been reported. Research integrity issues may affect far more researchers than currently recognized.
INDICATOR 3 OF 6
Self-Citations
Whether a researcher’s publications cite work by their own authors.
What it shows
	How many of a researcher’s publications are self-citations. A publication is a self-citation if at least one of its authors is also an author of at least one of its references.
	Turns orange when
	The researcher has no self-citations (with the caveats on who is analyzed, below).
	Source
	Dimensions publications data.
	WHICH PUBLICATIONS AND RESEARCHERS ARE INCLUDED
To ensure substantive engagement with the existing literature, only eligible publications are analyzed: review papers, research papers, research chapters and conference papers with a minimum of 9 references.
Only recently active researchers who are not completely junior are analyzed: those who published in the last 2 years and have a minimum of 5 eligible publications.
WHY IT MATTERS
Unless a researcher is very early in their career, their publications usually build on their own work or the work of their co-authors, so some self-citations are expected. The lack of any self-citations can be an indicator of paper mill use. More background is available here.
WATCH OUT FOR EARLY-CAREER RESEARCHERS
A researcher with fewer than 5 eligible publications, or no activity in the last 2 years, is outside the analysis. Do not read a missing self-citation result for them as a warning sign.
	INDICATOR 4 OF 6
Unusual Network Flag
Whether a researcher’s co-author network behaves unusually.
What it shows
	Whether the publishing pattern of a researcher’s co-author network is unusual. Researchers with a network flag (“Yes”) are approximately 100 times more likely to have been named as an author of a flagged publication.
	Turns orange when
	The researcher has an unusual network flag.
	Source
	Described in Porter & McIntosh (2024), https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-024-71230-8, and implemented for Dimensions data.
	HOW IT WORKS
Roughly speaking, this indicator checks the publication behavior of the network of co-authors of a given researcher. Normally many of these co-authors know each other and work together on other publications. If a researcher uses the service of a paper mill, their co-authors generally have few or no collaborations, since paper mills often randomly allocate their customers to the papers they produce.
WHY IT MATTERS
This is an indicator of paper mill activity. See Section 4, Indicator Statistics, for how common the flag is, and how often researchers with the flag also have flagged publications.
INDICATOR 5 OF 6
Co-Authors with Network Flag
How many of a researcher’s co-authors have the unusual network flag.
What it shows
	The number or percentage of co-authors that have the unusual network flag.
	Turns orange when
	The researcher has at least one co-author with an unusual network flag.
	Source
	As for indicator 4.
	WHY IT MATTERS
The network flag is conservative. Listing the percentage of co-authors that have it can show concerning practices by the selected researcher as well. See Section 4, Indicator Statistics, for additional insights.
INDICATOR 6 OF 6
Co-Authors with Flagged Publications
How many of a researcher’s co-authors have at least one flagged publication.
What it shows
	The number or percentage of co-authors that have at least one flagged publication.
	Turns orange when
	At least 20% of the researcher’s co-authors have flagged publications.
	Source
	As for indicator 2.
	WHY IT MATTERS
Not every problematic paper is caught with a retraction. Having collaborators involved in retractions may increase the likelihood that the researcher is involved in questionable practices as well.
THIS IS NOT A RARE FINDING
Having co-authors with flagged publications is common, mainly because researchers with flagged publications have a far higher number of co-authors. This is why the highlight starts at 20% of co-authors, not at just one. See Section 4, Indicator Statistics.
	SECTION 3
Where the Data Comes From
Author Check compiles data from various sources to find atypical events. Dimensions does not rely on just one source (e.g. Retraction Watch), as that may leave out a number of retracted or otherwise atypical publications.
FLAG TYPES
Flag
	What it means
	Retraction (notices)
	Issues with the publication have resulted in the publisher, editorial board and/or the authors withdrawing it. A retracted paper may or may not come with a retraction notice.
	Expression of concern
	Issues are being investigated. Although no final decision has been reached (e.g. a retraction), the publisher or editorial board has informed readers of potential problems.
	Tortured phrases
	Phrases that appear to be corruptions of established technical terms, with one or more words replaced by a synonym (e.g. “counterfeit consciousness” in place of “artificial intelligence”). Often the result of a paraphrasing algorithm used to avoid plagiarism detection.
	Citejacked
	The publication cites articles in hijacked journals, which use the ISSN, title and metadata of established journals on fake websites.
	

For more on tortured phrases, see this preprint and this Nature article. For more on citejacking, see this preprint.
WHERE THE FLAGS COME FROM
Source
	What it contributes
	Retraction Watch
	Its own database of retractions and expressions of concern, compiled from various sources and manually curated.
	CrossRef
	Mainly retractions and expressions of concern, usually when publishers update the metadata of their publication in CrossRef.
	PubMed
	Retractions provided by publishers.
	Dimensions
	Its own detection algorithm for retractions and retraction notices. This is needed because not all publishers update the necessary CrossRef metadata when a publication is retracted. Sometimes only the title changes, a retraction notice with a different DOI is issued, or a new version is created.
	Problematic Paper Screener
	Citejacked articles and tortured phrases.
	REASONS AND RETRACTION TAXONOMY
The Retraction Watch database also provides one or several short reasons for a retraction. To make this data easier to digest, Author Check maps it to the retraction taxonomy. You will see the reasons and taxonomy levels when you click a flag.
DATA BEHIND EACH INDICATOR
Indicator
	Data used
	Percentile of publication output
	Publications assigned to researcher profiles by Dimensions, and Dimensions’ field of research classification (ANZSRC). Includes preprints, books and book chapters.
	Flagged publications
	The sources listed above.
	Self-citations
	Dimensions publications data.
	Unusual network flag
	A method described in Porter & McIntosh (2024), implemented for Dimensions data. https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-024-71230-8
	Co-authors with network flag
	The unusual network flag, applied to each of the researcher’s co-authors.
	Co-authors with flagged publications
	Flagged publications, counted across each of the researcher’s co-authors.
	COVERAGE AND LIMITS
* Profiles. Indicators are calculated from the publications Dimensions has assigned to researcher profiles.
* Self-citations. The analysis covers only recently active researchers with at least 5 eligible publications.
* Reporting. The retraction process can be long and complicated, and many problematic papers have not been reported. Counts of publications per source are illustrative and are not regularly updated.
